NOVI(1)              UNIX Programmer's Manual              NOVI(1)



NAME
     novi - full-screen text editor

SYNOPSIS
     novi [ file ]

DESCRIPTION
     Novi is a small full-screen editor with nano-like key
     bindings.  If file is given it is read for editing; a name
     that does not exist starts an empty buffer under that name.
     With no argument, novi starts empty and ^O prompts for a
     name.

     The document is never held in memory.  Two scratch files
     under /tmp form a gap at the cursor: text before the cursor
     is written to one and text after it is written to the other
     in reverse, so both editing and cursor motion cost a bounded
     amount of memory whatever the size of the file.  Saving
     streams the two halves into a replacement file.  This is what
     lets novi edit a file larger than the 64K bank a task's data
     lives in; see the CAVEATS below for what it costs.

     The screen expects an ANSI/VT100-compatible terminal.  The
     window size comes from TIOCGWINSZ, falling back to 80x24.

COMMANDS
     Arrow keys move the cursor.  Home and End go to the start and
     end of the line, Page Up and Page Down move a screenful, and
     Insert toggles between insert and overwrite mode.  Insert
     mode is the default; overwrite replaces characters without
     consuming the newline that ends a line.

     ^A      start of line             ^E      end of line
     ^B      left                      ^F      right
     ^P      up                        ^N      down
     ^Y      page up                   ^V      page down
     ^D      delete character          ^G      help
     ^O      write the buffer out      ^R      insert a file
     ^W      search forward            ^K      cut a line
     ^U      uncut (paste)             ^C      report position
     ^X      exit

     Consecutive ^K's accumulate into the cut buffer; a ^K after
     any other command starts a new one.  ^U pastes the whole cut
     buffer at the cursor.

     ^X on a modified buffer asks before discarding it.

FILES
     /tmp/novi*      the two halves of the gap buffer
     /tmp/novicut*   the cut buffer
                     (each unlinked as soon as it is created)

SEE ALSO
     ed(1), elvis(1)

CAVEATS
     Every keystroke that moves the cursor moves a byte between
     the two scratch files, so novi's speed is the speed of the
     disk, not of the machine.  It is meant for editing files that
     do not fit in memory; ed(1) or elvis(1) are quicker for files
     that do.

     A save writes a temporary file beside the target and renames
     it over the original.  On a ProDOS volume the rename removes
     the original first, so an interruption at that moment loses
     the file.

     The scratch files keep the size they reached at their largest
     until novi exits.

     Novi has no undo.

